; bfsodium ROTL32 : rotate a 32 bit little endian word left by n
;
; HAND WRITTEN; It never shifts left  which is the whole point of the file: a
; left shift DOUBLES a byte and a doubling is idiom/add8  while a right shift
; HALVES one and a halving is a few instructions per unit of the byte; So a
; rotation left is done as whole BYTE turns  which are only moves  followed by
; one rotation right of fewer than eight bits;
;
; INTERFACE entry=4 exit=4 footprint=0:19
; IO  in:  w{4} LE  followed by  n{1}      (5 bytes)
;     out: (w rotated left by n){4} LE     (4 bytes)
;
; TAPE MAP  (home @0)
;   @0x00:0x03  w{4}   u8   the word  LSB at @0x00
;   @0x04       m      u8   n on the way in; then the RIGHT rotation that is
;                           left over  which is what idiom/rotr32 reads
;   @0x05:0x0c  idiom/rotr32's frame  pasted at this file's own zero
;   @0x0d       k      u8   whole byte turns still to make
;   @0x0e       s      u8   the bit part of n  which is n modulo eight
;   @0x0f       h      u8   HALVE frame: the byte being halved
;   @0x10       q      u8   HALVE frame: that byte shifted right one
;   @0x11       t      u8   HALVE frame: that byte low bit
;   @0x12       f      u8   HALVE frame scratch  restored to nought
;   @0x13       z      u8   one until the bit part is found not to be nought;
;                           LATER the byte that falls off the top of a turn
;
; THE IDENTITY  the same one idiom/rotl64 is built on:
;
;   rotl(w  8q plus s) = rotr(rotl(w  8(q plus 1))  8 minus s)     for s above nought
;   rotl(w  8q)        = q whole byte turns                        for s nought
;
; A whole byte turn is four moves of one cell and one of nineteen; nothing is
; doubled anywhere in this file;
;
; THE SECOND LINE IS A BRANCH AND idiom/rotl64 DELIBERATELY DOES NOT HAVE ONE
; which is worth explaining rather than looking like an inconsistency; rotl64
; is entered by Keccak's rho with twenty five different offsets and only three
; of them have s equal to nought  so the uniform formula wastes about four per
; cent there and a branch was not worth its else arm; ChaCha20 enters THIS file
; with four counts  sixteen twelve eight and seven  and TWO of them have s equal
; to nought; Skipping those two saves sixteen of the twenty one bit steps a
; quarter round would otherwise pay  so here the branch earns its keep several
; times over;
;
; MEASURED on one word per count  the four counts ChaCha20 uses:
;   sixteen  2636189 instructions  and now 40002
;   twelve   1948789               and now 189304
;   eight    1316645               and now 21835
;   seven    1170108               and now 61765
; which is 7071731 instructions for the four and now 312906  a little over
; twenty two times; IN PLACE that is chacha20/qrloop from 6641647 to 2236329
; and chacha20/blockloop from 686380403 to 331136996  so rotation was more than
; HALF of the block function and is now a small part of it;
;
; The count is on the wire rather than pasted in  because qrloop keeps the four
; counts in a table and turns it by one each step  so a step always reads the
; same cell;

; read w{0:3} then n  leaving the pointer on n @0x04
  ,>,>,>,>,
; ASSERT ptr=4
; ASSERT zero 5:19

; ==== split n into the byte part and the bit part ====
; n steps into the halving frame  which empties its cell for what goes back
  [-R11+L11]
R11
; ASSERT ptr=15
; the first halving drops bit nought of n  which is worth one in the bit part
  [->>>+<[-<+>>-<]>[-<+>]<<<]
R2
; ASSERT ptr=17
  [-L3+R3]
; what is left goes back to be halved again
L1
; ASSERT ptr=16
  [-L1+R1]
L1
; ASSERT ptr=15
; the second halving drops bit one  which is worth two
  [->>>+<[-<+>>-<]>[-<+>]<<<]
R2
; ASSERT ptr=17
  [-L3++R3]
; and back again
L1
; ASSERT ptr=16
  [-L1+R1]
L1
; ASSERT ptr=15
; the third halving drops bit two  which is worth four
  [->>>+<[-<+>>-<]>[-<+>]<<<]
R2
; ASSERT ptr=17
  [-L3++++R3]
; and the quotient that is left is n shifted right three  the byte part
L1
; ASSERT ptr=16
  [-L3+R3]

; ==== the right rotation is eight bits less the bit part ====
; to the counter rotr32 will spend
L12
; ASSERT ptr=4
  ++++++++
; the flag that says the bit part was nought  until the next loop says otherwise
R15
; ASSERT ptr=19
  [-]+
; the bit part is spent taking that count down
L5
; ASSERT ptr=14
  [-L10-R15[-]L5]

; ==== the word turns left one whole byte  q plus one times ====
L1
; ASSERT ptr=13
; the byte part is one turn short of what is wanted  because the right rotation
; above turns the word back by up to a whole byte
  +
; unless the bit part was nought  in which case there is no right rotation at
; all and the extra turn would be one too many
R6
; ASSERT ptr=19
[
  [-]
L6
; ASSERT ptr=13
  -
L9
; ASSERT ptr=4
  [-]
R15
; ASSERT ptr=19
]
; ASSERT ptr=19
L6
; ASSERT ptr=13
[
  -
; to the top byte of the word
L10
; ASSERT ptr=3
; it is kept while the three bytes below it move up into its place
  [-R16+L16]
; each byte takes the place of the one above it  from the top down  so that
; none of them is written over before it has moved
L1
  [-R1+L1] L1
  [-R1+L1] L1
  [-R1+L1]
; ASSERT ptr=0
; and the byte that fell off the top comes back in at the bottom
R19
; ASSERT ptr=19
  [-L19+R19]
; back to the count of turns
L6
]
; ASSERT ptr=13

; ==== and what is left is a right rotation of nought to seven bits ====
L13
; ASSERT ptr=0
; ASSERT zero 5:12
@@ROTR32@@ 0
; ASSERT ptr=0
R4
; ASSERT ptr=4
; ASSERT zero 4:19

; emit
; the rotated word little endian
L4
  .>.>.>.
